#edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557210 #edb/ref 1071 "A container with $\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons of weed killer can spray $\\frac{7}{4}$ lawns. What is the unit rate of gallons per lawn?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06090a54-3bf7-5dce-b7f7-51954f025453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557210 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of gallons per lawn, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{3}{4},$ by the number of lawns, $\\frac{7}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 4}{4\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{7}$ gallons per lawn." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-169441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557227 #edb/ref 1071 "Owen takes $\\frac{4}{5}$ hours to do $24$ math exercises. How many exercises can he do in $1$ hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557227 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of exercises Owen can do in $1$ hour, we compute the unit rate of exercises per hour. We do this by dividing the number of exercises Owen does, $24,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{4}{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n24÷\\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24÷\\frac{4}{5} & =24\\times \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\times 5}{1\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{4} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Owen can do $30$ exercises in $1$ hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557231 #edb/ref 1071 "At a fair, a carousel makes $3$ revolutions in  $\\frac{3}{5}$ minutes. How many revolutions does it make in $1$ minute?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557231 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out the number of revolutions the carousel makes in $1$ minute, we can compute the unit rate of revolutions per minute. We do this by dividing the number of revolutions, $3,$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{5}$, as follows:  \n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{3}{5} & =3\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the carousel makes  $5$ revolutions in $1$ minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557233 #edb/ref 1071 "A skydiver falls $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a mile in $\\frac{2}{5}$ minutes. What is the unit rate of miles fallen per minute?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557233 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{2}{5}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $2$ miles per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557262 #edb/ref 1071 "A certain satellite makes $\\frac{1}{3}$  of its orbit around the Earth in $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a day. How many times can it orbit Earth in $1$ day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557262 #edb/ref 1074 "To find how many  times the satellite  can go around the Earth in $1$ day, we can compute the orbital unit rate per day. We do this by dividing the amount of the orbit the satellite travels, $\\frac{1}{3},$ by the corresponding fraction of a day, $\\frac{1}{6},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the satellite can orbit Earth $2$ times in $1$ day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557263 #edb/ref 1071 "Riding his electric bike, Matt can go $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a mile in $\\frac{3}{4}$  minutes. What is the unit rate of miles covered per minute?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557263 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{1}{2},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 4}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557267 #edb/ref 1071 "A race car covers $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a lap in  $2$ minutes.  What is the unit rate of laps covered per minute?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70693bce-1563-5043-b145-f1732cc4b8df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557267 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of laps per minute, we divide the number of laps, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of seconds, $2$, as follows:     \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷2\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷2 & =\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 1}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{2}{5}$ laps per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557268 #edb/ref 1071 "Johnny walked $\\frac{3}{8}$ of a mile in  $7$ minutes, keeping an even pace. How many miles did he walk in $1$ minute?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71f0d135-eabe-57fb-ab96-cf20fb60fc0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557268 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the number of miles Johnny  walked in $1$ minute, we can compute the unit rate of miles per minute. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\frac{3}{8},$ by the number of minute, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷7\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}÷7 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 1}{8\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{56}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, Johnny walked  $\\frac{3}{56}$ miles in $1$ minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557279 #edb/ref 1071 "A plane can travel $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a mile in $6$ seconds.  What is the unit rate of miles traveled per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c31a83a-d0fb-50d8-8958-8a93c295b8d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557279 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per second, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{3}{5},$ by the number of seconds, $6$, as follows:    \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷6\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷6 & =\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 1}{5\\times 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 1}{5\\times 2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}\\times 1}{5\\times 2\\times \\cancel{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{1}{10}$ miles per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557296 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557296 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557296 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557296 #edb/ref 1071 "An air compressor moves $\\frac{8}{15}$ gallons of air in four seconds. Find the unit rate of gallons per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557296 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bde894c0-0f60-52a1-8f53-44362f2c04ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557296 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of gallons per second, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{8}{15},$ by the number of seconds, $4$, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{15}÷4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{15}÷4 & =\\frac{8}{15}÷\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\times 1}{15\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 4}{15\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times \\cancel{4}}{15\\times \\cancel{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{2}{15}$ gallons per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557306 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557306 #edb/ref 1071 "A leaking paddling pool loses $6$ liters of water in  $\\frac{2}{3}$ hours. How much water will it lose in $1$ hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557306 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out how much water the pool will lose in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of liters of water lost per hour. We do this by dividing  the number of liters, $6,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{2}{3}$, as follows:     \n\n$$\n6÷\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6÷\\frac{2}{3} & =6\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pool will lose $9$ liters of water in $1$ hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557332 #edb/ref 1071 "Suzie takes $\\frac{2}{3}$ hours to read $22$ pages of a certain book. How many pages can she read in $1$ hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557332 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of pages Suzie can read in $1$ hour, we compute the unit rate of pages per hour. We do this by dividing the number of pages Suzie reads, $22,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{2}{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n22÷\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22÷\\frac{2}{3} & =22\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{22\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{66}{2} \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Suzie can read $33$ pages in $1$ hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
